1
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,925
Hi. I'm Sue.

2
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:07,635
I'm a technical program manager

3
00:00:07,635 --> 00:00:09,775
for Google's support platform.

4
00:00:09,775 --> 00:00:13,290
A technical program
manager is responsible

5
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:15,480
for making sure the team

6
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:18,090
delivers on what
it sets out to do.

7
00:00:18,090 --> 00:00:21,375
That means pulling together

8
00:00:21,375 --> 00:00:24,940
a lot of different
capabilities and

9
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:28,310
deliverables coming
from across many teams

10
00:00:28,310 --> 00:00:30,275
to pull together a product

11
00:00:30,275 --> 00:00:32,120
that we deliver to our customers.

12
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:33,890
Without customers,

13
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:37,790
there's really no reason
to be building anything.

14
00:00:37,790 --> 00:00:40,730
And what's interesting about building

15
00:00:40,730 --> 00:00:43,970
projects and working with
customers and trying

16
00:00:43,970 --> 00:00:46,715
to imagine what they want,

17
00:00:46,715 --> 00:00:50,255
is it puts you in this
really unique place

18
00:00:50,255 --> 00:00:53,675
where you need to put
yourself in their shoes

19
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:55,760
and imagine the world or

20
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:58,280
imagine the product
through their eyes.

21
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:00,365
In a project management role,

22
00:01:00,365 --> 00:01:02,495
being able to empathize and

23
00:01:02,495 --> 00:01:05,600
understand how different
people in the world will

24
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:07,850
interact with your
product is really

25
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:11,715
important and gives you
this amazing perspective.

26
00:01:11,715 --> 00:01:14,330
Understanding the
customer's needs

27
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:16,130
is a very difficult job,

28
00:01:16,130 --> 00:01:18,005
but it's really important.

29
00:01:18,005 --> 00:01:20,870
I believe it's a skill you
can develop over time.

30
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:23,029
I actually consider a customer

31
00:01:23,029 --> 00:01:25,565
just a certain kind
of stakeholder.

32
00:01:25,565 --> 00:01:27,710
I like to take my stakeholders

33
00:01:27,710 --> 00:01:29,780
and group them into buckets,

34
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:32,540
so I have customers,
and they are usually

35
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:34,490
the end users of a product or

36
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:36,920
the ones that are
purchasing the product.

37
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:40,051
And then I have stakeholders
that are sponsors,

38
00:01:40,051 --> 00:01:41,630
rather—the people that are funding

39
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:44,075
the team that's
actually building this.

40
00:01:44,075 --> 00:01:48,230
Those two groups of people
are very different, and I like

41
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:52,510
to treat a sponsor in
a very special way.

42
00:01:52,510 --> 00:01:58,005
They are looking to increase
their return on investment,

43
00:01:58,005 --> 00:02:01,210
and so you always need to
be looking for ways to

44
00:02:01,210 --> 00:02:03,505
quantify, how are you

45
00:02:03,505 --> 00:02:06,580
generating a return
on that investment?

46
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:09,190
There's always this little fear

47
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:11,410
that they're not going to
get what they paid for,

48
00:02:11,410 --> 00:02:15,985
and giving them
information frequently

49
00:02:15,985 --> 00:02:18,070
and at the right
level of detail is

50
00:02:18,070 --> 00:02:20,790
a really important skill to
have as a project manager.

51
00:02:20,790 --> 00:02:23,110
Sometimes you can
overwhelm them with

52
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:25,660
too many details and they
cannot see the forest

53
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:27,910
through the trees,
and sometimes you

54
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:30,550
don't give them enough
detail, and they don't

55
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:32,630
trust what you're
telling them. And so

56
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:35,660
striking that balance
is really important.

57
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:38,570
I really enjoy seeing
a project from

58
00:02:38,570 --> 00:02:42,170
the very beginning to the
very end, when you actually

59
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:44,210
get to show it to a customer and

60
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:46,430
get their acknowledgment and

61
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:48,875
recognition and see
their excitement

62
00:02:48,875 --> 00:02:51,350
at seeing the product
actually working;

63
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:53,375
something that, when
you just started,

64
00:02:53,375 --> 00:02:57,050
there was nothing—just
ideas and maybe some papers.

65
00:02:57,050 --> 00:02:59,780
Then, however many months

66
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:03,005
later you actually have a
product that you can show off.

67
00:03:03,005 --> 00:03:05,810
What I love about project
management is it puts you in

68
00:03:05,810 --> 00:03:07,550
that role, so you can really

69
00:03:07,550 --> 00:03:10,620
see things from the
beginning to the end.
